        <mxCell id="HQQ4V66wD_OCbyy4vYDD-69" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;text-align: left; margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Key Generation For Short Url&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;All keys for shour urls will be pre-generated by key generation service (KGS).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Key length is 6 chars. Using base64 encoding system, I can get: 64^6 = 68,7 billion keys&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;I&#39;ll use PostgreSQL (no partitioning, with replication) database with very simple two tables:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;After jey retrieved by other services, it will be moved to used_keys table.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;I&#39;ll run 2 instances of this service for redundancy, in case one service goes down.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Also we cache 10K urls in redis, so clients can get key immediately. There can be a situation when we lose some keys if redis goes down. However it is okay in our case, since we have way more unique keys than we need. We do not need partitioning here, since 10K keys are not big amount of data.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;However we want to replicate our data for fault tolerance and read latency.&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="300" width="490" x="600" y="440" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="HQQ4V66wD_OCbyy4vYDD-70" parent="1" style="shape=table;startSize=30;container=1;collapsible=1;childLayout=tableLayout;fixedRows=1;rowLines=0;fontStyle=1;align=center;resizeLast=1;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;" value="keys" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="60" width="180" x="620" y="560" as="geometry" />
